From: Project Luma
Subject: [PATCH] luma: apps appear as soon as they open

Window memory keeps a window with a remembered place invisible until it is
there. The short reveal timer was armed only by Mutter's "shown" signal, so
an X11 client that mapped and drew without "shown" arriving after the hold
began (an Electron app through the container launcher took 12 s) stayed
invisible until the 10 s safety timeout, and Wayland apps waited about a
second on the same path.

Any sign that the window reached the screen now counts as shown: "shown",
the actor's first frame, damage, or the actor being mapped, including when
it already is at the start of the hold. Each arms the reveal timer, now
250 ms, and the hold ends at the latest 1 s after it began. A window that
is not in place by then is shown where it is and moved to its place with
Tiling Shell's usual animation. Every reveal logs how long the window was
held and what showed it; a hold over 500 ms is logged as a warning.
---
--- a/components/lumaWindowMemory/windowMemory.js
+++ b/components/lumaWindowMemory/windowMemory.js
@@ -42,10 +42,16 @@ const DEFAULT_TIMING = {
   // after a restore, Mutter and clients settle before recording resumes
   quiet: 1000,
   launchDelay: 120,
-  // a window held invisible until it is in its place is shown anyway this long
-  // after the client first shows it, or this long after it was created
-  holdAfterShown: 700,
-  holdMax: 10 * 1e3,
+  // A window is held invisible only until it is in its place, and never
+  // noticeably: it is shown anyway this long after it first reaches the screen
+  // (Mutter's "shown", its first frame, damage or mapping), and at the latest
+  // this long after the hold began. Placement usually settles within a few
+  // frames; a window that has not by then is shown where it is and moved to its
+  // place afterwards with the usual animation.
+  holdAfterShown: 250,
+  holdMax: 1000,
+  // a hold longer than this is logged as a warning, so a regression shows up
+  holdWarn: 500,
   // a stay shorter than this is a detour: it adds hours but not a session
   dwell: 60 * 1e3,
   // open windows credit their hours this often, so a crash loses little
@@ -93,7 +99,7 @@ function stateFile() {
 }
 
